  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{10+3{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10+3{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}}=\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0, \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{s}} & =5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}}+3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =5{t}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{10+3{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}}$ is $f(t)=5{t}^{2}+3.$",
